Investigation evidence chain — SideCamera AC-4 timing — revision 3

Scope: the accepted single-camera AC-4 max-gap failure. The original five-minute reproduction is reused; no capture was rerun for revision 3. Observed facts are listed separately from inference.

1. Observed — accepted five-minute current-source capture (2026-11-15 08:15:26–08:20:31 UTC). 6,050 matched frames over 302.506666 s; 6,049 EOF intervals averaged 49.998692 ms (20.000523 fps), max 89.163715 ms, with 753 intervals over 60 ms. This remains an AC-4 max-gap failure. Investigation `evidence/reproduction.log` is byte-identical to `../build/evidence/runtime-smoke-ac4-r2.2-round2.log`, SHA-256 `de804d35326c6925862d2c8b36708d4a14a10b05120076924f523397976e8c55`. The current `system/sidecamerad.py` hash remains `494fb8b88fda8bcf1d8301225019f4f01f4b77a0bb5cb3e69dde47ed96e1309b`.

2. Observed — corrected same-run DQBUF / PTS / write-boundary / read-completion trace (2026-11-15 10:00:10 UTC). After startup, DQBUF intervals: 600, mean 49.994824 ms, max 58.634 ms, zero over 60 ms. SOE PTS intervals: 598, mean 50.006737 ms, max 52.128 ms, zero over 60 ms. Raw output write-boundary intervals: 598, mean 49.957065 ms, max 89.580 ms, 146 over 60 ms. Full-frame read-completion intervals: 598, mean 49.957056 ms, max 89.587 ms, 147 over 60 ms. Write-start to read completion was median 0.116 ms and p95 0.196 ms. There were 602 successful DQBUF calls for 600 output frames, and the raw ioctl trace omitted V4L2 sequence fields; exact dequeue-to-output identity is not proven. Build report SHA-256 `6d96162d197305e68eb88c8ae29cd8f67f957930c627ce01d4d8932318336c5a`; artifact `../build/artifacts/dqbuf-output-correlation-r2.3-r1.json` SHA-256 `29c587a2bf121c8e35c16481400f621686505cf3ad3afe0f95cb7f0ffab130b2`; raw trace `../build/evidence/v4l2-dqbuf-ffmpeg-output-r2.3-r1.strace` SHA-256 `5eb1ed5f3f73c01d88e6745a2c4c7df170f9d0324319ac374c1f4a84b98fd1b5`.

3. Observed — output syscall durations in the corrected DQBUF trace. Across 25,800 writes, median 0.058 ms, p95 0.153 ms, p99 0.256 ms, max 10.554 ms, none over 50 ms. The separate writes-only trace was observer-intrusive and unpaired with DQBUF; it is corroborating evidence only.

4. Observed — 600-frame untraced read with per-thread `/proc/<pid>/task/*/schedstat` snapshots (2026-11-15 10:20:43 UTC). 600/600 frames, 599 read intervals, 77 over 60 ms, maximum 80.383947 ms. Four FFmpeg threads exposed schedstat. Long-gap intervals had 1.469 ms mean total runqueue delay, 0.988 ms mean maximum single-thread delay; only 3/77 had any thread delay >=5 ms. Other intervals had 1.273 ms and 0.848 ms means, with 13/522 >=5 ms. This does not support CPU runqueue delay as the primary cause. Post-read `/proc` sampling adds cost to the following interval and does not identify the exact writer thread. Saved JSON `artifacts/ffmpeg-schedstat-600-r2.json` SHA-256 `e3bea2a7874fdb5e6b98d6aeb7fbaeca65308845feb684883d8491e11b01aa36`.

5. Observed — sequential output-flush A/B with one variable, `-flush_packets 1` (default arm began 2026-11-15 11:03:40 UTC; flush arm began 11:04:10 UTC). Both traced arms completed 600/600 frames and 25,800 writes; no payload was saved. Source hash stayed unchanged. Default arm: read gaps 122/599 >60 ms, max 88.425002 ms; write-boundary start gaps 120/599 >60 ms, max 88.367 ms. Flush arm: read gaps 107/599 >60 ms, max 86.679440 ms; write-boundary start gaps 106/599 >60 ms, max 86.696 ms. Thus the single sequential pair showed 15 fewer read gaps over 60 ms and a 1.746 ms smaller read maximum; write-boundary count fell by 14 and maximum by 1.671 ms. Both arms still had substantial >60 ms gaps. Write syscall durations had no values over 60 ms (max 9.632 ms default; 11.402 ms flush). Exact build report SHA-256 `f6e9f0a2a172d9401e28627d180823bc806bd1f6c1e1627789a489f513902c06`; JSON `../build/artifacts/ffmpeg-flush-pacing-ab-r2.3-r1.json` SHA-256 `913a57ae11491fe31ee103884b98a2c1df357b9d8dbc8f8c89c519f0d1786f3c`; default trace `../build/evidence/ffmpeg-flush-default-r2.3-r1.strace` SHA-256 `8de6089eff1a1557e552d325e8dd051774485451d69ad953c40c3ee6be1506fb`; flush trace `../build/evidence/ffmpeg-flush-packets-1-r2.3-r1.strace` SHA-256 `cfde2108693799f2f87222ff8058a6fededba3c955c4ee09288e973f3ca4bc75`; summary log `../build/evidence/ffmpeg-flush-pacing-ab-r2.3-r1.log` SHA-256 `510294d1de93851644766d6fd7394f836abd8bdba5b3059d38507b1f5e72717f`. Both traces used strace, were sequential and unpaired, so this small difference is not causal proof and can include observer, order, and run-to-run effects. No production argv change was made.

Inference (medium confidence): cadence jitter remains localized after V4L2 dequeue and inside FFmpeg processing/output before raw-pipe delivery. Reader backlog, CPU runqueue delay as the primary cause, and sustained pipe backpressure are not supported. The small one-pair flush difference is inconclusive; it does not establish output flushing as the cause or a fix. Exact internal FFmpeg decode/filter/output pacing or synchronization wait remains uncertain. PTS are V4L2 SOE, not EOF; keep `timestampEof` as host monotonic time after complete raw-frame read.
